Below are concise, ready-to-use templates and practical tips for requesting a copy or verification of your Social Security Number (SSN) from the issuing authority (in the U.S., the Social Security Administration). I won’t repeat the full SSN you provided — I’ll show how to refer to it safely (using only the last four digits).

Before you send anything
- Prefer secure channels: do not send your full SSN over unencrypted email. Use SSA’s secure portal (mySocialSecurity), in-person visit, or certified mail when possible.
- Check whether you can use the online replacement card service (mySocialSecurity) — that’s often the fastest.
- Gather required ID: typically one or more government IDs (driver’s license, state ID, U.S. passport) and documents proving citizenship or lawful presence (birth certificate, immigration documents). If your name changed, include name-change documentation.
- SSA replacement cards are usually free; confirm with the local office.
- If you’re not in the U.S., tell me your country so I can tailor wording.

Short email template (for SSA/local office contact form)
Subject: Request for Social Security number verification / replacement card

Hello,

I am requesting assistance to obtain verification of my Social Security Number (or a replacement card) for the purpose of [e.g., completing a form, employment verification, tax filing]. My details are:

- Full name: [Your full legal name]  
- Date of birth: [MM/DD/YYYY]  
- Last four digits of SSN: 1234  
- Current address: [Your address]  
- Preferred contact method: [phone/email/mail]

Please let me know what documentation you require and the next steps to complete this request, and whether this can be handled securely online or if I must visit my local office in person.

Thank you,  
[Your name]  
[Phone number]  
[Optional: mySocialSecurity username or account email]

