- Do not include your full SSN in email or online forms unless you are on an official, secure government site (e.g., ssa.gov) or dealing directly with a verified office. Instead refer to it as “SSN ending in 1234.”
- If you’re requesting a replacement card or proof from the Social Security Administration (SSA), the SSA usually requires identity documents and the SS‑5 form (or in-person/secure online application). Replacement cards are free. Check ssa.gov for the current process and office locations.

Below are ready-to-use templates you can adapt depending on whether you’re sending a letter/email or calling.

1) Formal letter (mail or upload to SSA office)
