3) Phone script (calling SSA or local office)
“Hello — my name is [Name]. I’m calling because I need a replacement Social Security card / official verification of my SSN. My SSN ends in 1234. My date of birth is [DOB]. What documents do I need to bring or send, and how do I submit the request? Is there an appointment available at the local office, or can I do this online?”

What to attach or bring (typical):
- Proof of identity: U.S. driver’s license, state ID, or U.S. passport (original or certified copies may be required)
- Proof of citizenship or immigration status if applicable (birth certificate, certificate of naturalization, or immigration documents)
- Completed Form SS‑5 for replacement cards (available at ssa.gov) if required
- Any other supporting documents requested by SSA

Timing and other tips:
- Replacement cards typically arrive by mail within about 10–14 business days after the SSA accepts the application, but check current timelines.
- If you need documentation for an employer or a government agency, ask whether they will accept a photocopy/printout or require an official SSA verification letter.
- Never send your full SSN in an unsecured email or to an unverified address. Use ssa.gov or go in person to a local SSA office when possible.

